  "content": {
    "id": "p22-q22-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "22",
    "prompt": "Find the coefficient of $x$ in the expansion of $\\left(x+\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^5\\left(x-\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^4$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$6$",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\left(x+\\frac1x\\right)^5\\left(x-\\frac1x\\right)^4&=\\left(x+\\frac1x\\right)\\left[\\left(x+\\frac1x\\right)\\left(x-\\frac1x\\right)\\right]^4\\\\&=(x+x^{-1})(x^2-x^{-2})^4.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $(x^2-x^{-2})^4$, the general term is\n$$\\begin{align*}T_r&=\\binom4r(x^2)^r(-x^{-2})^{4-r}\\\\&=\\binom4r(-1)^{4-r}x^{4r-8}.\\end{align*}$$\nMultiplication by $x+x^{-1}$ produces an $x$ term from the constant term and the $x^2$ term of this expansion.\n\nFor the constant term,\n$$\\begin{align*}4r-8&=0\\\\r&=2.\\end{align*}$$\nFor an $x^2$ term,\n$$\\begin{align*}4r-8&=2\\\\r&=\\frac52.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $r$ must be an integer from $0$ to $4$, there is no $x^2$ term. Therefore the required coefficient is\n$$\\binom42(-1)^{4-2}=6.$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 50
  },
